✅✅ Location / Geospatial Indexing ✅✅
QuadTree · Geohash · S2 · H3 · PostGIS
(H3 in detail — used in Promise Engine. Rest kept short.)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🎯 The Problem ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

"Find drivers / stores / users within 5 km of me"
"Which delivery zone is this lat/lng inside?"

A point = (latitude, longitude)
latitude  : -90 to +90   (north/south)
longitude : -180 to +180 (east/west)

1° latitude  ≈ 111 km (everywhere)
1° longitude ≈ 111 km × cos(latitude)  → at Delhi (28.6°) ≈ 97.5 km

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 1️⃣ Naive: Store lat/lng, Scan Everything ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

user_locations
user_id | latitude | longitude
1       | 28.6139  | 77.2090     (Delhi)
2       | 28.6120  | 77.2100     (Delhi, ~200 m away)
3       | 19.0760  | 72.8777     (Mumbai)

Search:
for each user:
    if haversine(user, me) < 5 km: include

🔹 Haversine (distance on a sphere, R = 6371 km)
a = sin²(Δlat/2) + cos(lat1)·cos(lat2)·sin²(Δlng/2)
d = 2R · asin(√a)

❌ 10 million users → 10 million rows scanned + 10 million haversine calls. DB dies.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 2️⃣ Bounding Box + B-tree Index ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Me = (28.6139, 77.2090), radius 5 km
Δlat = 5 / 111    = 0.045°
Δlng = 5 / 97.5   = 0.051°

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE latitude  BETWEEN 28.569 AND 28.659
  AND longitude BETWEEN 77.158 AND 77.260;
Then exact haversine only on these rows (box corners are > 5 km).

✅ Scans a small region
❌ A B-tree range-scans ONE dimension only.
   INDEX(latitude, longitude) → range on latitude stops the index there.
   So it returns the whole horizontal strip (28.569–28.659 across all of India),
   and longitude is filtered row by row.
❌ Doesn't scale to Uber-level.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🧠 The Core Trick: Turn 2D into 1D ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Every approach below does one of two things:
1. Build a spatial tree        → QuadTree, R-tree (PostGIS / MySQL SPATIAL)
2. Cut the Earth into cells    → Geohash, S2, H3
   point → cell ID (string / int) → a normal B-tree / Redis / HashMap can index it

The standard nearby-search pattern (all grid systems):
point → my cell → my cell + neighbour cells → fetch candidates → exact haversine filter

Grids give CANDIDATES. The final distance check is always exact.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🌳 QuadTree (short) ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Take a box (whole map). If it holds more than N points (capacity), split into 4:
+------+------+
|  NW  |  NE  |
+------+------+
|  SW  |  SE  |
+------+------+
Keep splitting only where data is dense.
Delhi with 1 lakh drivers → very deep. Desert with 0 → never splits.

🔹 Which child does a point go to?
midLat = (minLat + maxLat) / 2
midLng = (minLng + maxLng) / 2
lat >= midLat and lng <  midLng → NW
lat >= midLat and lng >= midLng → NE
lat <  midLat and lng <  midLng → SW
lat <  midLat and lng >= midLng → SE

🔹 Insert
insert(node, point):
    if node is leaf and has space: add point
    else:
        if leaf: split into 4, push existing points down
        insert(correct child, point)

🔹 Search
Point search  → one child per level, like a BST walk
Radius search → visit only nodes whose box intersects the search circle, skip the rest

✅ Adapts to density (no space wasted on empty areas)
✅ Great for static data: restaurants, landmarks, POIs, heatmaps, game collision
❌ Moving objects (drivers every few sec) → constant delete + re-insert, re-splits
❌ Can go deep and unbalanced (points on a line / tight cluster)
❌ A pointer tree in memory → hard to shard across machines

Where it lives: in-memory (RAM) index, GIS engines, games.
Databases mostly use R-trees (bounding rectangles), not QuadTrees.
You rarely code it yourself.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ #️⃣ Geohash (short) ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Alternate binary cuts: longitude, latitude, longitude, latitude...
left/bottom half → 0, right/top half → 1
Every 5 bits → 1 Base32 char ("0123456789bcdefghjkmnpqrstuvwxyz")

Real values:
Connaught Place, Delhi (28.6139, 77.2090) → ttnfucjbh
Noida Sector 18                            → ttnfx9...
Mumbai                                     → te7ud2...

🔹 Longer string = smaller cell. Shared prefix = same bigger cell.
chars | cell size (approx)
4     | 39 km × 19.5 km
5     | 4.9 km × 4.9 km
6     | 1.2 km × 0.6 km
7     | 153 m × 153 m
8     | 38 m × 19 m
Delhi CP and Noida share "ttnf" → same 4-char cell.

⚠️ Edge problem (real example)
Two points 5.9 m apart across a cell border:
ttnfuc000  vs  ttnfu9pbp   → only "ttnfu" in common
So never query just your cell → query your cell + its 8 neighbours.

❌ Rectangles, and they get narrower as you move away from the equator
❌ 8 neighbours, but corner ones are farther than edge ones (1.41×)
❌ Z-order curve jumps → nearby cells can have far-apart strings

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🧊 S2 — Google (short) ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Put a cube around the Earth → 6 faces → project the sphere onto them.
Each face is quad-split recursively (like a QuadTree), levels 0–30.
Level 0 = a whole cube face. Level 30 = about a centimetre.

🔹 Cell ID = 64-bit integer
[3 bits face (0–5)] + [2 bits per level (child 0/1/2/3)] + [marker bit]
Path example: face 2 → 1 → 3 → 0 → 2

🔹 Hilbert curve ordering (the important bit)
Cells close on Earth → IDs close as numbers (better locality than geohash's Z-order).
So a region becomes a few ID RANGES → cheap B-tree range scans.

🔹 Radius search
1. Circle (center, 3 km)
2. RegionCoverer → a small set of cells, mixed sizes, that cover the circle
3. Fetch rows WHERE s2_cell_id BETWEEN range_min AND range_max (per covering cell)
4. Exact haversine filter

drivers(driver_id, lat, lng, s2_cell_id at a chosen level, updated_at)

✅ Children tile the parent EXACTLY (H3's don't)
✅ Best at covering arbitrary shapes with few cells
❌ Square-ish cells, uneven neighbours, harder to reason about
Known users: Google Maps, MongoDB 2dsphere index, Tinder geosharding.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ ⬡ H3 — Uber (DETAILED · used in my project) ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Uber's open-source hexagonal grid over the whole Earth (2018).
Any lat/lng + a resolution → exactly one hexagon ID.
Libraries: h3-js (Node, what we used: ^4.2.1), h3-py, core in C.

🔹 How the grid is built
Icosahedron (20 triangles) around the Earth
→ 122 base cells at resolution 0 (110 hexagons + 12 pentagons)
→ each cell splits into ~7 children per resolution ("aperture 7")
→ 16 resolutions: 0 (continent) … 15 (about a square metre)
12 pentagons at EVERY resolution, all placed over oceans → irrelevant for India.

🔹 Why hexagons
Square grid (geohash / S2):
+---+---+---+
| C | E | C |     E = edge neighbour, distance 1
+---+---+---+     C = corner neighbour, distance 1.41
| E | X | E |     two kinds of neighbour → uneven rings
+---+---+---+
| C | E | C |
+---+---+---+

Hexagon grid:
    / \ / \
   | N | N |
  / \ / \ / \     every neighbour shares an edge
 | N | X | N |    all 6 centres at the SAME distance
  \ / \ / \ /     → expanding k steps looks like a circle
   | N | N |
    \ / \ /

✅ 6 equal neighbours, round-looking rings, near-equal cell areas
❌ You can't cut a hexagon into 7 smaller hexagons exactly
   → parent/child containment is APPROXIMATE (children spill slightly over the parent's edge)
   → If you need exact hierarchy, use S2. Using ONE fixed resolution avoids the issue.

🔹 Resolution table (from h3-js getHexagonEdgeLengthAvg / AreaAvg / NumCells)
res | avg edge | avg area      | cells on Earth | feels like
7   | 1.41 km  | 5.16 km²      | 98.8 M         | a neighbourhood
8   | 531 m    | 0.74 km²      | 692 M          | a few blocks
9   | 201 m    | 0.105 km²     | 4.84 B         | a city block
10  | 75.9 m   | 15,048 m²     | 33.9 B         | a building complex   ← we used this
11  | 28.7 m   | 2,150 m²      | 237 B          | one building
Each resolution down = ~7× more cells, edge ÷ ~2.65 (√7).
Res 10: adjacent hexagon centres are ~131 m apart (√3 × edge).

🔹 Anatomy of a cell ID (real)
Connaught Place, Delhi (28.6139, 77.2090) at res 10:
h3.latLngToCell(28.6139, 77.2090, 10)  →  "8a3da11462f7fff"

A 64-bit integer, printed as 15 hex chars:
0 | 0001 | 000 | 1010 | 0011110 | 110 100 001 000 101 000 110 001 011 110 | 111 111 111 111 111
│    │     │     │        │        └─ 10 digits (0–6), one per resolution  └─ unused digits = 7
│    │     │     │        └─ base cell = 30 (of 122)
│    │     │     └─ resolution = 10  (hex 'a' → every res-10 cell starts with "8a")
│    │     └─ reserved
│    └─ mode = 1 (a cell)
└─ reserved
Digits: 6 4 1 0 5 0 6 1 3 6 | 7 7 7 7 7   → the unused 7s give the trailing "fff".

Parents = same bits, resolution field changed, deeper digits set to 7 (bit ops, no geometry):
res 9 → 893da11462fffff
res 8 → 883da11463fffff
res 7 → 873da1146ffffff
res 5 → 853da117fffffff

🔹 Core API (h3-js v4 name   [old v3 name])
latLngToCell(lat, lng, res)             [geoToH3]         point → cell
cellToLatLng(cell)                      [h3ToGeo]         cell → centre
cellToBoundary(cell)                    [h3ToGeoBoundary] cell → 6 vertices (draw on a map)
polygonToCells(coords, res, isGeoJson)  [polyfill]        polygon → cells whose CENTRE is inside
gridDisk(cell, k)                       [kRing]           cell + everything within k steps
gridDistance(a, b)                      [h3Distance]      steps between two cells
cellToParent / cellToChildren           [h3ToParent / h3ToChildren]
compactCells / uncompactCells           [compact]         7 full children → 1 parent

gridDisk size = 3k² + 3k + 1  →  k=1: 7 cells, k=2: 19 cells

