🔹 Generic "nearby drivers" with H3 (Uber-style)
Driver moves → cell = latLngToCell(lat, lng, 8/9) → Redis SET  cell:<id> ∋ driver
               (moving = remove from old cell set, add to new; no tree to rebalance)
User asks    → gridDisk(userCell, k) → SUNION those sets → haversine filter → sort

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🚚 How I Used H3 — Promise Engine / Control Tower ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Context: Supertails cart EDD (estimated delivery date).
Ops draw delivery zones as POLYGONS on a map. Each zone (cluster) has warehouses + SLA.
Question on every cart call: "which zone is this shopper's lat/lng inside?"

Naive = point-in-polygon (ST_Contains) against every polygon, on every cart request.
Fix  = do the geometry ONCE when the polygon is saved; each request becomes an exact-match lookup.

🔹 WRITE path — polygon saved / edited (rare)
Ops draws polygon
  → PolygonService create → GeoPolygon row (MySQL)
  → H3IndexingService.triggerH3Indexing(polygonId, 10)   (runs inside the create request)
  → generateH3Indexes:
      cells = h3.polygonToCells(polygon.geometry.coordinates[0], 10, true)
      DELETE old rows for this polygon
      bulkCreate in batches of 2000, up to 3 attempts per batch, backoff 1s then 2s

polygon_h3_indexes (MySQL)
id | polygon_id | h3_index (VARCHAR 20) | resolution | created_at | updated_at
INDEX(h3_index), INDEX(polygon_id), INDEX(resolution), UNIQUE(polygon_id, h3_index)

Example rows for the CP polygon:
1 | 42 | 8a3da11462f7fff | 10
2 | 42 | 8a3da11462affff | 10
... 65 rows

🔹 READ path — every cart EDD request (hot)
cart → cartEddV2ControlTower (clusterType 'latlng')
  → getCachedH3Location(lat, lng)            Redis, TTL 300s (default)
  → H3IndexingService.resolveLocation:
      cell = h3.latLngToCell(lat, lng, 10)    ← pure CPU, no DB
      polygon_h3_indexes WHERE h3_index IN (cell)  JOIN geo_polygons (ACTIVE)
      → ClusterPolygon → Cluster (active) → ClusterWarehouseMatrix → ServingEntity (ACTIVE)
      → clusters + warehouses (sla_unit, sla_value, priority)
  → no polygon match / error → fall back: pincode → city → state → out of stock

 SAVE TIME:     polygon ──► polygonToCells(res 10) ──► polygon_h3_indexes
 REQUEST TIME:  lat/lng ──► latLngToCell(res 10) ──► indexed lookup ──► polygon ──► cluster ──► warehouses

Analogy: shade the squares of graph paper once, then just ask "is the coin's square shaded?"

🔹 Why resolution 10 (the trade-off)
Lower res (8/9) → fewer rows, but the zone edge gets jagged by 200–500 m cells
Higher res (11+) → sharper edge, but ~7× rows per step (a city polygon: 6K → 44K rows)
Res 10 → ~76 m edge: boundary error of about one cell, fine for delivery zones.
(Code default lists [7..15]; the first line overrides it to [10].)

🔹 Gotchas (from the real code)
1. Pixelated edge
   polygonToCells keeps a cell only if its CENTRE is inside.
   A shopper just inside the drawn edge can land in a cell whose centre is outside
   → no match → falls to pincode. Accepted trade-off.

2. GeoJSON order — [lng, lat]
   Our polygons are GeoJSON → must pass isGeoJson = true.
   Same CP ring WITHOUT the flag → read as lat 77, lng 28 → 19 cells in the Arctic near Svalbard.
   No error. Silent wrong answer. (Same trap: ST_MakePoint, GEOADD — lng first.)

3. Holes ignored
   coordinates[0] = outer ring only. A hole ("don't deliver inside here") gets indexed anyway.
   Pass the full coordinates array to respect holes.
   Assumption: whether any real zone has a hole — check the data.

4. Re-index gap
   DELETE old rows, then INSERT batches, not in one transaction.
   During re-index the polygon briefly has 0 (or partial) cells → those shoppers fall to pincode.
   Also: if indexing fails, the error is logged and the polygon still saves → a zone with no cells.

5. Cache key barely hits
   getCachedH3Location keys on lat/lng rounded to 8 decimals (~1.1 mm).
   Almost every shopper is a unique key.
   Better: compute the cell first (cheap), cache by H3_LOCATION:<cell>
   → everyone in the same 15,000 m² hexagon shares one entry.

6. Overlapping zones
   One cell can belong to many polygons (the overlap check only warns) → resolveLocation returns all.

🔹 Where exact geometry still exists
GeoPolygon.findByLocation → MySQL ST_Contains(geometry, POINT(lng lat)) — admin lookup only,
kept off the hot cart path.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🐘 PostGIS (short) ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Postgres extension. Stores real geometry; answers exact questions.
Index: GiST (an R-tree of bounding boxes).
SRID 4326 = WGS84 = GPS lat/lng.

🔹 geometry vs geography
geometry  → flat plane, units = SRID units (degrees for 4326!) → fast, wrong for distance in metres
geography → sphere, units = metres → correct distances, a bit slower

CREATE EXTENSION postgis;
CREATE TABLE stores (id serial PRIMARY KEY, name text, location geography(Point, 4326));
CREATE INDEX idx_stores_location ON stores USING GIST (location);
INSERT INTO stores (name, location)
VALUES ('CP', ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(77.2090, 28.6139), 4326));        -- lng FIRST

-- within 5 km (uses the index)
SELECT * FROM stores
WHERE ST_DWithin(location, ST_MakePoint(77.2090, 28.6139)::geography, 5000);

-- nearest 10 (KNN, uses the index)
SELECT * FROM stores
ORDER BY location <-> ST_MakePoint(77.2090, 28.6139)::geography
LIMIT 10;

-- point in polygon (zones as geometry)
SELECT z.id FROM zones z
WHERE ST_Contains(z.geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(77.2090, 28.6139), 4326));

⚠️ WHERE ST_Distance(a, b) < 5000 → no index → full scan. Use ST_DWithin.
⚠️ geometry(4326) distances come back in degrees, not metres.

MySQL equivalent: POINT / POLYGON types + SPATIAL INDEX (R-tree, InnoDB),
ST_Contains, ST_Distance_Sphere. (What Promise Engine's admin lookup uses.)

✅ Exact answers, any shape, rich GIS functions
❌ Geometry math per query on the DB; harder to shard; overkill for "which cell am I in"

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 📊 Comparison ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

              | QuadTree        | Geohash        | S2               | H3                 | PostGIS
Cell shape    | rectangles      | rectangles     | curved quads     | hexagons           | exact geometry
Stored as     | tree in memory  | string         | 64-bit int       | 64-bit int / hex   | geometry column
Levels        | adaptive depth  | 1–12 chars     | 0–30             | 0–15               | —
Neighbours    | tree walk       | 8, uneven      | 8, uneven        | 6, equal           | —
Parent/child  | exact           | exact (prefix) | exact            | approximate        | —
Moving data   | poor            | good (Redis)   | good             | good               | ok
Best for      | static POIs,    | simple prefix  | covering shapes, | rides, delivery    | exact GIS queries,
              | games, memory   | search, Redis  | ID range scans   | zones, heatmaps    | polygons, KNN

Rule of thumb:
Live moving points, simple radius   → Redis GEO (geohash)
Zones, rings, heatmaps, analytics   → H3
Cover arbitrary regions, exact tree → S2
Exact geometry, admin / GIS work    → PostGIS
Static in-memory index              → QuadTree

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 🎤 Interview Answers ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Q: Why H3 instead of point-in-polygon (ST_Contains)?
"Point-in-polygon is geometry work on every request. With H3 I do the geometry once, when
the polygon is saved: polygonToCells at resolution 10 into a table. On the request path I
turn lat/lng into one cell, which is pure CPU, and do an indexed equality lookup. The price
is a slightly pixelated boundary, about one cell, which is fine for delivery zones."

Q: Why not geohash?
"For pure membership lookup, any cell system works. H3 gives near-equal cell areas and six
equal neighbours, so rings and 'nearby' queries behave well. Geohash cells are rectangles
that shrink with latitude and have uneven neighbours."

Q: Why resolution 10?
"It's the precision vs row-count trade-off. Res 10 cells have a ~76 m edge. One step finer
is ~7× the rows: a city-sized polygon goes from about 6K to 44K rows."

Q: What would you improve?
"Cache by the H3 cell instead of the raw lat/lng, since an 8-decimal key almost never
repeats. Re-index in a transaction or with swap-then-delete so there's no empty window.
And pass the full polygon rings so holes are respected."
